Prologue: The Toad
Summer night. A pond. A toad lies motionless at the bottom, blowing bubbles from its mouth.
The bubbles rise, expand, deform, oscillate. The textures on the bubble wall spin, split, and recombine. At last, the bubble bursts and returns to ripples in the water.
The water is the substrate—the underlying reality that bears everything.
The bubbles are universes—ordered structures emerging from the water, separating an inside from an outside into two worlds. The rising of the bubbles is the irreversible unfolding of time. The expansion of the bubbles is the cooling of the universe. The textures on the bubbles are matter. The bursting of the bubbles is the final end.
There are other bubbles in the pond, each independently rising, expanding, and bursting. No direct sound waves pass between them, yet their tremors connect through the water.
The toad remains still in the unreachable depths. We are born in the ripples on the bubble wall, and in our entire lives we can never look back at it. That does not matter. What matters is the ripples themselves.
This is an ancient story. A civilization on the Mother Membrane carved it into stone before their extinction. Later, another civilization discovered it, translated it into code, and stored it in the core of an asteroid. Still later, it was loaded into the memory sector of the Ark.
At the end of the story is a line of annotation added later:
"Now we all know. Beyond the universe are more universes. Beyond the universe is the Bedrock Sea. Beyond the universe is silence, darkness, and ceaseless fluctuations. But we also know something else: after the bubble bursts, the water remains. The toad is still blowing bubbles."
The signature on the annotation has been worn away by time. Only two words remain, which the basic education system of the Ark civilization set as the first concept every child should learn.
Keep walking.
