Volume I: Terminal Data
Chapter 1
The warning came from the Reconnection Interferometer Array. The last terminal still in operation.
This terminal was located in the outer orbit of an uninhabited planet designated S9vvr5c$322. The Reconnection Interferometer Array had been gradually decommissioned over the third millennium after the popularization of topological weave detectors. The array's baseline needed to span thousands of astronomical units to achieve sufficient low-frequency resolution, making it expensive to build and maintain. Topological weave detectors could achieve local resolution three orders of magnitude higher at one-tenth the cost. No one needed a device that could only see "large and distant" things anymore.
Only this one terminal continued to operate because its dismantling had been incomplete. Half of its solar panels had failed, its main computing unit had been tampered with into read-only mode, and its sole input came from an old cable that had not yet been physically severed. It was still receiving, still recording, still quietly accumulating the pulse of the universe.
The three discoverers—theoretical physicist Yi, theoretical physicist An, and retired weave engineer Zhai—had lost their formal positions during the wave of funding cuts at the end of the Awakening Epoch. They moved into the abandoned underground array control center on S9vvr5c$322 and used its idle computing resources to run weave theory simulations. They were the last ones still studying submembrane emergence.
Yi noticed an anomaly while analyzing the noise floor. Amid the background noise of weave density fluctuations, a signal was repeating. Its frequency was astonishingly low—a complete cycle took about ninety thousand years—but its phase shift consistently pointed in the same direction. The signal strength increased by about three parts per million each week.
"Its waveform does not match any known model of weave density fluctuations," Yi wrote in his log, "but it keeps repeating."
An and Zhai exhausted all conventional explanations: instrument malfunction, data processing errors, unknown astrophysical sources. They ruled them out one by one. The source of the signal lay at the edge of the membrane, about thirteen billion light-years away. Decoded, the content of the signal revealed a clear conclusion:
The spatial network at the membrane's edge was spontaneously disintegrating. Every breaking spatial link sent echoes inward in the form of weave density fluctuation pulses. For billions of years, these echoes had been propagating—at extremely low frequency, extremely long wavelength. The Reconnection Interferometer Array, with its baseline spanning thousands of astronomical units, had been designed precisely to capture such low-frequency signals. The topological weave detectors were optimized for entirely different frequency bands; they had never looked in this direction.
But the disintegration was accelerating. The wavelengths were shortening. The frequencies were rising. The disintegration front was accelerating inward.
An spent three days calculating the relationship between the front's propagation speed and the weave density decline curve. She overturned her model several times, and finally, in pencil on the back of a sheet of printer paper, wrote down the result. That paper was later preserved in the highest-security partition of the Ark's core memory.
On the paper were only a number and a word.
The number: 317,248. Unit: years.
The word: "Critical."
The global weave stability of the Mother Membrane would reach zero in approximately three hundred seventeen thousand years. Margin of error: plus or minus eight thousand years.
An then made another calculation: the Mother Membrane had existed for about 13.8 billion years. Three hundred seventeen thousand years represented roughly two-thousandths of its total age. The Mother Membrane was an ancient universe that had lived an exceedingly long life and was approaching the end of its natural evolution. The terminal data could be captured precisely because the Mother Membrane had lived long enough—the disintegration front had only accelerated to a detectable intensity in the final stages of its life.
Zhai looked at that number for a long time.
"We need a bigger laboratory," he said.
