Chapter 2
Three hundred seventeen thousand years. A blink on the cosmic scale. An extreme constraint on the engineering scale.
The first rough estimate was completed within forty hours after the terminal data was confirmed. Building a submembrane Ark capable of housing at least ten billion individuals would require a total amount of elementary particles equivalent to the sum of all matter in about twelve thousand Milky-Way-class galaxies. The energy required would be roughly one hundred trillion times the current civilization's annual energy consumption.
Intergalactic transport alone, using conventional intra-membrane methods, would take billions of years. The Ark was physically impossible to build.
But this estimate contained a hidden assumption: that transport must occur within the intra-membrane spatial network. Zhai overturned it at the forty-first hour.
"We won't transport inside the membrane," he said. "We'll transport in the substrate."
The core insight was as follows: the speed of light inside the membrane is merely a property of the spatial network. The conduction velocity of reconnection pulses in the substrate is about eleven orders of magnitude higher than the intra-membrane speed of light. Any material—elementary particles obtained by dismantling stars—is essentially a weave structure. Weave structures can be pushed into the substrate, transmitted within the substrate in the form of reconnection pulses, and then pulled back into the membrane at the destination.
This is not transporting matter. This is scan-transmit-reassemble. Particles at the point of departure are dismantled into weave information; the information travels instantly to the destination via substrate pulses; at the destination, a local particle reservoir is used to re-weave them. The particles at the departure point are recycled into the local reservoir for the next reassembly. Particles circulate in the process—the weave pattern is conserved, and the particles themselves are merely carriers.
Yi and An completed the feasibility argument within the following days: it was physically valid. The engineering difficulty was extreme, but it violated no known constraints of weave theory. The velocity of substrate signal propagation was so many orders of magnitude larger that the distance between any galaxies approached zero in terms of pulse transmission. The real bottleneck was no longer transportation, but the scanning rate and reassembly rate.
The Central Planning Matrix reran the optimization algorithms. The new construction timeline estimate: 220,000 to 280,000 years.
On the fortieth day, a document marked "Priority: Existential" was simultaneously transmitted to all of the more than seven hundred member galaxies of the Local Group. Its filename, meaning "Survival" in the language of the time, was later translated as "The Ark Project."
The document's first paragraph read:
"The universe we inhabit is dying. An irreversible global weave disintegration is occurring at the membrane's edge. The disintegration front is advancing inward at the intra-membrane speed of light. Based on the current propagation speed and acceleration trend, all weave structures of the Mother Membrane will be reduced to zero in approximately 317,000 years. The Mother Membrane has existed for about 13.8 billion years and is approaching the end of its natural evolutionary lifespan. The time window is inelastic. Construction must begin now."
That same day, the complete universal star chart was loaded into the Central Planning Matrix. This star chart had been accumulated by the civilizations of the Local Group over hundreds of millennia through substrate pulse surveys—the precise coordinates, mass distributions, and material compositions of two trillion galaxies, all on record. The Planning Matrix's task was not "exploration," but "screening." Among the two trillion known galaxies, it would delineate the core civilization areas to be preserved, and then designate the optimal dismantling targets.
The algorithm ran for about seven hours. The output was as follows:
Core Preservation Zone: Centered on the Milky Way and the Andromeda Galaxy, encompassing about fifty nearby civilized galaxies that had established stable intergalactic ties. These galaxies would be directly embedded within the closed surface of the Ark's hull, serving as the main structural interior space of the Ark.
Material Supply Zone: Approximately twelve thousand galaxies surrounding the Core Preservation Zone. Optimal transport distance; material compositions meeting all constraints for hull weaving. To be dismantled in their entirety.
The remaining approximately 199 million-plus galaxies in the Mother Membrane were not on the dismantling list. They were too far away. They would naturally disintegrate along with the Mother Membrane in 317,000 years. The Ark had no capacity to save them, nor to destroy them. No causal connection would be established between them and the Ark within the remaining lifespan of the Mother Membrane.
The first dismantling fleet launched that same day. Its commander was an elderly structural engineer surnamed Ke. He gave a final communication before departure.
"You are about to go dismantle the first target galaxies. What do you want to say?"
"We are going to collect materials."
"What is your view of the Ark Project?"
"From an engineering perspective, feasible. From a survival perspective, the only option. Both assessments are simultaneously true, and do not contradict each other."
Ke did not live to see the Ark completed. In the third year after departure, his fleet completed the dismantling of the first batch of masquerade stars, and the materials were instantly transmitted back to the construction zone via substrate pulses. Ke died of natural aging in the fourth year. His successors carried out his unfinished tasks. After the Ark was completed, the core construction zone was named the "Ke Domain"—a spherical space about five million light-years in diameter, where Ke's fleet had dismantled the first masquerade stars all those years ago. The galaxies there had been dismantled completely, leaving only a dark zone with no luminous celestial bodies. Later generations called it "Ke's Graveyard."
In the first millennium after the Ark Project was launched, the core task of civilization was to reorganize itself. A political entity that integrated the more than fifty civilized galaxies of the Local Group into a unified will gradually took shape—the "Construction Compact." Its full name was "The Resource Mobilization and Allocation Compact Established to Address the Ultimate Cosmic Termination," which no one ever used. It was simply the Compact.
The Compact's establishment was not accompanied by ethical debate. The moment the terminal data was confirmed, the time for debate had already ended. The first plenary session of the Compact Council lasted only one day. Its minutes contained only one line: "Unanimously agreed to execute the Ark Project. No votes against. No abstentions."
