Volume II: Construction
Chapter 3
The Ark's official designation was "Project First Submembrane." Construction engineers called it "the Hull," planners called it "the Final Structure," and mathematicians called it "the Closed Surface Solution."
The Ark was not a ship. It was an independent small universe—a closed weave surface that emerged inside the Mother Membrane and independently maintained weave stability. The effective interior space of the hull was roughly equal to the volume of the Local Group. The more than fifty civilized galaxies, centered on the Milky Way and Andromeda, were completely embedded inside the hull, serving as the main structural body of the Ark. The hundreds of billions of stars within these galaxies, and the planetary systems orbiting them, were preserved intact within the hull.
The night sky for the Ark's inhabitants had two layers of starlight. The nearer layer was the stars inside the Ark—their own suns, the starlight of neighboring galaxies, the familiar constellations of the Local Group. The farther layer was the Mother Membrane universe outside the hull—galaxies, clusters, and the cosmic web on scales of tens of billions of light-years. The hull was a perceptual "glass window"; the light of the Mother Membrane could pass through, until the day of severance.
The gravity grid was the skeleton of the Ark's internal spatial structure. Early in the construction, Zhai designed the optimal space-filling pattern: within the given closed surface, maintaining the maximum number of stars in stable orbits with the minimum total energy. The hundreds of billions of stars did not rotate around a single massive center; they were distributed, each placed precisely at a calculated gravitational lattice point, pulling and balancing one another. The entire Ark was a vast gravitational loom.
Zhai spent three hundred years solving the mathematical form of this space-filling pattern. He died the year after completing the design. Over the next four thousand years, his design was woven into the structure of the hull by successive generations. When the hull was completed, Zhai had been dead for over ten thousand years.
The radius of the Ark's closed surface was determined by two joint factors: the surface defect fraction and the expected voyage timescale. The defect fraction had to be suppressed to far below the level of naturally emergent membranes—below one part per million. The total elementary particles required to achieve this weave density was the sum of twelve thousand galaxies. This figure came from a multi-variable joint optimization of the minimum curvature radius of the closed surface, the relationship between surface defect density and particle count, and the expected voyage timescale.
In the Mother Membrane universe, masquerade stars were the most crucial material source for the Ark's construction. Masquerade stars were not artificial structures—they were a class of naturally occurring extreme celestial bodies: an outer shell of plasma undergoing thermonuclear fusion, with luminosity and spectrum indistinguishable from ordinary stars; and a core that was a primordial supermassive black hole. A masquerade star was not a star heading for death—from the moment of its birth, its core was already a black hole. In the Mother Membrane universe, the number of masquerade stars was about one-thousandth of the total stellar population—a vast natural material reservoir on a galactic scale. Dismantling a single masquerade star yielded a particle quantity equivalent to dismantling an entire star cluster.
Yi died forty years after the launch of the Ark Project. In his last technical memorandum, he wrote: "We need to build a universe. A small universe, but still a universe. The Mother Membrane lived 13.8 billion years just to wait for us. Our Ark needs to live even longer. Over its long life, the Mother Membrane has already prepared the materials for us. We are not slaughtering the universe. We are merely cutting down a tiny corner of an unimaginably vast forest. That corner happens to be the patch where we live. For those trees that were cut, it is a total, one-hundred-percent end. But for the entire forest, they will not leave even a gap that could be seen from afar."
A cosmic-scale lifespan required cosmic-scale materials. The materials were already marked on the star chart. The countdown had begun.
