Chapter 4
The Construction Compact's dismantling procedure was divided into four steps.
Step one: deploy the gravitational lens array.
Step two: controlled restructuring of stellar orbits.
Step three: the dismantling itself. The dismantling of an ordinary star took about twelve to eighteen hours; its luminosity gradually rose to hundreds of billions of times its normal brightness, then decayed sharply over a few hours. After dismantling, all matter was converted into discrete elementary particles.
The dismantling of masquerade stars was a different procedure. First, strip the outer gas shell—the plasma layer that had sustained thermonuclear fusion for hundreds of millions of years was peeled away by gravitational lenses within hours, releasing light pulses visible from planets tens of light-years away. Then the black hole core was exposed—a lightless region surrounded by a bent ring of light. The gravitational lens array dismantled it layer by layer starting from the event horizon. The particles inside the black hole had already been compressed by gravity to theoretical limits; after dismantling, the resulting particle density was thousands of times greater than that of ordinary stars.
A black hole left no remnants. After dismantling was complete, only a dispersing cloud of discrete particles remained in the original sky region. In the night sky of nearby planets, it was a star that suddenly brightened ten-thousandfold and then vanished over a few days.
Step four: substrate pulse transmission. The particles were scanned, encoded, transmitted to the construction zone at substrate signal speed, and reassembled there using a local particle reservoir. The transmission delay approached zero.
A complete galactic dismantling cycle standardly took about three to seven days.
During those three to seven days, any intelligent civilization in the target galaxy could observe the anomalies—if they happened to be observing. They could analyze the nature of the anomalies—if their physics had advanced to that stage. But they could make no effective response. The deployment of the gravitational lens array was completed in minutes. By the time their most advanced observatory confirmed that "stellar structures are disintegrating," that signal had already traveled for centuries. By the time they sounded the alarm, their sun had already gone out. The time-lag of interstellar communication became an absolute cage in the face of dismantlement.
The passive receivers on the fleet automatically recorded all electromagnetic signals from within the target galaxy—as part of the standard data acquisition procedure. These signals included the communications, broadcasts, and final reactions of the native civilizations. At the peak of construction, hundreds of galaxies were dismantled every day, and tens of thousands of such signals flooded the data servers daily. No one had time to listen to each one.
These signals were later stored en masse in a partition of the Memory Sector. The partition's name: "Horizon."
