Chapter 5
At an observatory on a nameless planet at the edge of the construction zone, a child recorded another version with her own eyes.
The child's name has not come down to us. In the archives, there is only a log entry by the observatory technician on duty that day. It reads: "Local dusk. An underage female resident entered the observation dome. Unauthorized. Not ejected. She witnessed the entire dismantling of star KF-447MO2. Duration: 14 hours, 17 minutes. During this time, she did not speak or eat. After it ended, she left the dome. Destination unknown."
That star's third planet had oceans of liquid water. Primitive algal life. An atmosphere. Clouds. Over the course of fourteen hours and seventeen minutes, that planet's sky went from azure to incandescent white, from white to deep red, from red to pitch black. The clouds were vaporized, the oceans boiled, the atmosphere escaped into space. Those algae—the only life forms that had evolved in that one corner of the Mother Membrane—all died in the seventh hour. They had no consciousness, no civilization, did not know the universe was ending, did not know why their sun was suddenly brightening. They simply died.
What that child witnessed was not the dismantling of a star. She saw the end of a world. Not through data, not through theory, but through the transparent wall of the dome—that star swelled before her eyes, brightened, became so bright that the dome's light filters couldn't dim it fast enough, flooding the observation chamber with daylight. Then it began to shrink. Shrink. Shrink. Finally it became a small dark-red dot, like a piece of burnt-out charcoal. And then it went out.
When she left, the observing technician added a note to the log: "She paused at the door, looked back at the data on the screen. The star was marked 'Dismantling complete, particle recovery rate 99.97%.' She looked at that number for a while, then left."
This log was later seen by an engineer from the Dismantling Bureau. He wrote a line beside it in pencil. The handwriting was scrawled, almost illegible.
"99.97%. The remaining 0.03% was the world she saw."
