Chapter 6
During the construction, one chief dismantling director deserves to be recorded. His name was Gu Lin.
After Ke's death, Gu Lin took over the dismantling operation. By then construction had entered its peak phase; the masquerade stars in the Ke Domain were mostly dismantled, and the next wave of targets began expanding outward. The Central Planning Matrix issued a new list of priority dismantling galaxies, sorted by transport distance, mass concentration, and dismantling energy cost.
On the universal star chart, the information of every galaxy was transparent. Whether an intelligent civilization existed within a target galaxy, its technological level, its population distribution—all were clearly visible on the chart. The Planning Matrix's sorting algorithm did not read this information. It read only engineering parameters.
But the people carrying out the dismantling would see.
Gu Lin saw on the list a galaxy—NGC-447SS2-S7—whose chart annotation indicated it contained an intelligent species at an early technological stage. Alternatives existed. Another galaxy on the list was about eight million light-years farther than S7, with slightly higher transport energy cost and a schedule extension of roughly nine hundred years. These numbers were all within the safety margin.
Gu Lin submitted the alternative to the Compact's Resource Mobilization Committee. He used purely engineering language: the stellar density in S7's core region was too high; concentrated dismantling could trigger uncontrollable large-scale gravitational collapse; he recommended dismantling the alternate target first.
The committee deliberated for three days. The final vote passed the alternative. It was a slim majority.
After the vote, Gu Lin returned to his office and changed S7's marker on the star chart from "Awaiting Dismantling" to "Bypass." He added a note to S7's file: "This galaxy contains intelligent life. By vote of the Compact Council, this galaxy is permanently removed from the dismantling sequence. Signed, Gu Lin."
Then he began processing the next galaxy on the list.
Gu Lin did not live to see the Ark completed. When he died, S7 was still quietly rotating billions of light-years away. The people of S7 would never know that, somewhere in the distant universe, a man named Gu Lin had once cast a vote for them.
