Chapter 7
When construction entered its final phase, the authority to confirm dismantling orders was transferred to the third chief dismantling director. Her name was Shen Yuan.
Shen Yuan was a student personally mentored by Gu Lin. She inherited his star chart, his work logs, and that line of note he had left in S7's file. She inherited his silence.
In the final thirty thousand years of construction, the vast majority of the twelve thousand galaxies in the Material Supply Zone had been dismantled. About four hundred targets remained. These galaxies were marked on the star chart as the "Closure Sequence"—the final batch; once they were dismantled, the Ark's hull could be sealed. Shen Yuan's duty was to confirm the dismantling order of these galaxies and to sign her name on every dismantling directive.
The star chart was transparent. She could see the internal situation of each target galaxy. Of the four hundred galaxies awaiting dismantling, more than three hundred contained intelligent civilizations. Her signature meant the termination of those civilizations.
She had no right to refuse to sign. The countdown had thirty thousand years left. The Ark's safety margin in the closure phase had already been compressed below the theoretical limit—any delay in the supply of every ton of particles would directly translate into a drop in hull weave density. She could not refuse. The Compact Council had clearly told her: the Closure Sequence list was the final result of 230,000 years of optimization; no alternatives were available. Her signature was merely a formality.
She signed. Her name was on the dismantling directive for every one of those galaxies. Over three hundred times.
But in the final year, the Central Planning Matrix gave her an option she had not anticipated.
The last masquerade star to be dismantled—designated KD-9A901—was in an isolated region between two galaxy clusters. The mass of this masquerade star was immense; dismantling it would provide enough particles to complete the final segment of the hull's weave. The Planning Matrix simultaneously offered another option: do not dismantle KD-9A901, and instead dismantle seventeen surrounding ordinary star systems of lower mass. Those seventeen systems contained no masquerade stars; dismantling them was more difficult, total energy consumption would be about five-thousandths higher, and the schedule would be extended by roughly four hundred years. A four-hundred-year extension was a borderline value within the hull's safety margin—acceptable, but no further delays could be tolerated.
Shen Yuan pulled up the star chart annotations for both options.
The galaxy containing KD-9A901 harbored seven civilizations that had already achieved interstellar travel capability. These seven highly developed civilizations shared the resources of the masquerade star's galaxy and had established complex interstellar political systems, with a total population in the trillions. Within the standard work cycle of a dismantling fleet, all civilizations in this galaxy would be reduced to a cloud of discrete particles in three to seven days.
The other seventeen galaxies also contained civilizations, but their combined numbers were less than one percent of those in the KD-9A901 galaxy.
Either dismantle one galaxy, exterminating seven highly advanced civilizations. Or dismantle seventeen galaxies, exterminating a larger number of civilizations of much smaller scale.
Both options were viable in purely engineering terms. The Planning Matrix listed both as "Items Awaiting Decision," noting their respective energy costs, timelines, and risk parameters. Its sorting algorithm did not read civilizational data. It did not know it was making Shen Yuan choose whom to kill.
Shen Yuan sat in her office for two days. Her assistant later wrote in a memoir: "During those two days, she saw no one. She only repeatedly reviewed the star chart annotations for both options. The seven civilizations of KD-9A901—their languages, histories, records of recent interstellar political disputes—she read them all. She also read the civilizational data of the other seventeen galaxies. After finishing, she shut down the terminal. She did something no one in the history of the Dismantling Bureau had ever done: she made her choice not based on engineering parameters, but on the number and scale of civilizations. She chose to dismantle KD-9A901."
Shen Yuan signed the dismantling directive for KD-9A901. Beneath the directive, she added a handwritten note:
"This galaxy contains seven confirmed intelligent civilizations. Upon execution of dismantlement, all will be terminated. The signatory of this directive has been informed of this information. No alternative is available. The signatory assumes full responsibility."
She wrote this note into the formal directive. It was the first time in the history of the Dismantling Bureau. Previously, in all dismantling directives, the existence of civilizations had appeared only in the engineering data appendix. She placed it in the body of the directive.
KD-9A901 was dismantled. The seven civilizations all ended within four days.
Shen Yuan died about two thousand years after the Ark's launch. Before her death, she destroyed all her personal journals and left only a brief letter. The letter was addressed to Gu Lin—even though Gu Lin had been dead for over two hundred thousand years.
"Teacher, you wrote 'permanently removed from dismantling sequence' in S7's file. I signed over three hundred dismantling directives, each meaning the end of one or more civilizations. In the final directive, I faced a choice between seven civilizations and many more smaller ones. I chose to dismantle the seven. Not because I thought they were unimportant. It was because I didn't know how to choose. I wrote down their names. Seven of them. I remembered them all my life. My successors will probably also remember my name, remember me as the person who signed more than three hundred directives of civilizational termination. I don't know if this counts as taking responsibility. Probably just memory."
The letter was never sent. It was preserved in Shen Yuan's personal file. The archive classification: "Unsent Private Correspondence."
