Chapter 8
In the final phase of construction, a matter affecting the fate of ten billion people needed to be decided: Who would board the Ark?
The Ark could only hold a limited number of individuals. The total population of the Mother Membrane civilization at the Compact's peak was about one hundred quintillion. The Continuation Sector inside the Ark was built to living-person habitation standards, housing approximately ten billion individuals. The Memory Sector was built to information preservation standards, thousands of times larger in scale, meant to store the legacy of civilization rather than living beings.
The final "Dual-Track Plan" divided the Ark's interior into the Continuation Sector and the Memory Sector. The selection of boarding qualifications was performed by an algorithm, taking into account three dimensions: knowledge contribution, genetic diversity, and psychological adaptability.
One of the designers of the selection system was a young structural algorithm engineer. His name has been redacted from the Ark's core memory. For narrative convenience, call him Chen Du.
Chen Du had never imagined his mathematical tools would be used to decide who lived and who died. He accepted the Compact's commission and mathematically proved that this algorithm could achieve "maximum civilizational continuation value." For this, he felt a kind of cold pride.
Then his own name appeared in the output results. Not on the selection list. His wife, not on the list. His daughter, not on the list. His father, not on the list.
Chen Du sat in his office the whole night. Then, using his system administrator privileges, he accessed the weight matrix. He modified three parameters. Each modification fell within the algorithm's normal optimization tolerance range, and each had a purely technical justification. Combined, the four people he needed to cover all entered the selection interval.
Logically unassailable. No audit could detect "cheating." But he himself knew.
In the final year before launch, Chen Du received a selection notice. His wife and daughter received notices the same day. His father received his on the third day. The notices all began with the same line: "You are invited to participate in the continuation of civilization."
Chen Du's wife looked at the notice in his hand and remained silent for a long time. She was a scholar of ancient Mother Membrane languages, spending her life with obscure knowledge that was never meant to be selected for "maximum continuation value." She knew she should not be on the list.
"You did it?" she asked.
Chen Du did not answer.
She looked at him for a long time, then averted her gaze. She did not ask again.
On the eve of the launch, Chen Du stood alone on the open plain. Above him were two layers of stars—nearby, the stars inside the Ark, neatly arranged by the gravity grid; far away, the universe of the Mother Membrane, vast, chaotic, stretching into unknowable depths. He found that patch of sky he knew best. There was a galaxy there where he had done postdoctoral research in his youth. It would not board the Ark. It would turn into reconnection pulses when the Mother Membrane disintegrated. No one would remember it.
Had anyone there looked up at the starry sky? Had anyone, on some summer night, compared the universe to a bubble blown by a toad?
He did not know. He only knew that, axiomatically, he was wrong. But as a human being, he had passed. He had cheated. He had saved four people. He had not been selected—he was the one who chose. That distinction was everything.
At the edge of the firmament, a patch of starlight seemed to dim a little.
He told himself it was an illusion.
Chen Du died about forty years after the Ark's launch. His wife had passed away a few years earlier. They lived quietly into old age. On his deathbed, Chen Du said to his wife: "I stole four places."
His wife looked at him. "You think I didn't know?"
That is another story.
