Volume III: Launch
Chapter 10
The exact date of the construction's completion is disputed—because "completion" is a vague concept. The stitching of the final module was finished around the year 317,000 of the Mother Membrane Era, about 248 years before the disintegration time predicted by the terminal data.
Then came a long testing period. Three topological isolation tests verified the Ark hull's ability to independently maintain weave stability. The Ark was its own universe.
Boarding took decades. The ten billion individuals of the Continuation Sector were distributed across more than fifty galaxies. Transporting them all to the Ark required the "life-compatibility mode" of substrate pulse transmission; the error rate had to be kept below ten to the power of negative fifteen. Boarding was completed within about forty years. Zero transmission accidents.
The Mother Membrane's disintegration came one hundred and twenty years later than predicted.
An's last unpublished manuscript before her death had discussed this possibility: the membrane's disintegration was a kind of slow oscillation of weave density near the critical point. On the last page of the manuscript, she wrote: "The Mother Membrane may linger near the critical point for a long time. Having lived 13.8 billion years, it will not leave in a hurry at the very end."
One hundred and twenty years. The Ark waited one hundred and twenty years.
One day, after those one hundred and twenty years, the Mother Membrane began to disintegrate. The disintegration did not start at the edge, but from some random region in the interior. The shockwave propagated outward at the intra-membrane speed of light, met the disintegration front advancing from the edge years later, and covered the entire Mother Membrane.
The Ark received a reconnection signal warning about seven days before the shockwave arrived. The last connecting bridge to the Mother Membrane was placed in a ready-to-sever state. Severance was completed in the first microsecond within the three-second window of the shockwave's arrival.
That day, all the people across the Ark walked out onto the open plains.
No notice, no broadcast needed. Anyone who looked up understood.
Some of that eternal starry sky disappeared, never to appear again!
—those distant, dim ones, the ones you had to squint to see. The galaxies, star clusters, the web-like glow of the cosmic web on scales of tens of billions of light-years—all of them, simultaneously, from every direction—vanished.
The Mother Membrane disintegrated; the galaxies emitted their final light.
At the instant the shockwave arrived, the hull severed the last connecting bridge. The vast river of stars outside the window—the entire 13.8-billion-year starlight of the Mother Membrane universe—returned to darkness in a single night.
On the plains, people tilted their heads back and silently watched the sky.
The remaining stars were blindingly bright. Those were the stars inside the Ark. They were still there. They knew nothing. The gravity grid had arranged them in perfect order. In the night sky no longer illuminated by the Mother Membrane's starlight, the regularity of the grid was fully exposed to the naked eye for the first time—a vast, three-dimensional, luminous crystalline structure hanging overhead. Precise. Silent. Bone-deep beauty.
That was the geometry left by Zhai. He had been dead for two hundred and thirty thousand years, yet the gravitational field he designed still operated. Every point of light was in the position he had calculated.
Some knelt. Some wept. Some laughed—that irrepressible laugh when you realize you are being tenderly carried by someone from two hundred thousand years ago.
An old engineer held his grandson and pointed to the sky: Look, this is our home. Before, there was light outside. Now there is only our own light. But look how orderly this light is. Grandpa Zhai arranged it for us.
On the Ark's internal communication channels, reports came in from planet after planet: All external Mother Membrane starlight observed to have extinguished. Confirmed. Confirmed. Confirmed.
The final report came from a remote observation planet. The observatory duty officer added a sentence to the report—not standard format, just an ordinary sentence.
"The Mother Membrane's last heartbeat has stopped."
That phrase would later circulate inside the Ark for tens of millions of years. No one questioned its technical accuracy. It was the instinctive reaction of a civilization witnessing the death of another universe.
The voyage log recorded: At the moment of severance, the hull's outer-surface monitoring system registered a brief signal—of an unclassifiable nature. A simple, repeating, gradually decaying pulse sequence. It lasted about seven seconds. Then it vanished completely.
The core analysis system in the Memory Sector ran for thousands of years, trying every possible encoding hypothesis, without reaching a definitive conclusion. It was informally named "The Mother Membrane's Last Heartbeat." 13.8 billion years. Seven final seconds. A beginning and an end.
Yi's body, hundreds of thousands of years ago, had been dismantled into elementary particles and woven into the baseline layer of the spatial network of the Continuation Sector's central ecological unit. He chose to remain among the living. "An guards the memory," his will read, "I guard the people who remember."
An's body was woven into the core storage nodes of the Memory Sector. Her tombstone was the entire Memory Sector.
Zhai left no remains. He asked to be woven into the first module of the hull. His ashes are the keel of the Ark.
________________________________________
Volume IV: Voyage
Chapter 11
The first few millennia of the Ark's voyage within the substrate were later called the "Era of Silence."
The Ark's propulsion method was entirely different from intra-membrane transport. In the substrate, the Ark generated directional thrust pulses by actively regulating the distribution of surface defects on the hull—releasing controlled sequences of reconnection events into the substrate and gaining thrust through recoil. Its cruising speed was about one hundred-millionth of the substrate signal velocity, a value that still vastly exceeded the intra-membrane speed of light.
But at this speed, the external world was perceptually completely silent.
The hull was no longer a transparent "glass window." Outside the window was no longer the starlight of the Mother Membrane—it was now the Bedrock Sea, without light, without matter, only unceasing reconnection impacts. The hull had gone from window to wall.
Perception shifted from "seeing" to "touching." Stress sensors on the inner surface of the hull converted each external reconnection impact into data, and the data into a visualized tactile image—a constantly changing, unstructured, grey-tone image, displayed on the sky dome screens in the public spaces of the Continuation Sector.
The residents gave it a name: "The Surface of the Sea." The sea was the Bedrock Sea, and the surface was their only contact with it.
Watching this image gradually became a daily ritual in the Continuation Sector. People still watched. For them, watching was touching, and touching was confirmation—confirming they were still there, confirming there was something outside, confirming that the Ark was not traveling alone in the void.
In the third millennium of the Era of Silence, the Ark's course passed through the Ke Domain—that five-million-light-year dark zone. A night-shift observer, during a routine check of external monitoring data, noticed a direction that had absolutely no reconnection impact signals.
He called up the star chart and found that the outline of that region perfectly matched Ke's survey records from back then. He sat alone in that observatory for a long time. In that field of his duty log, he wrote only one word: "Seen."
His supervisor, reviewing the log, saw the word, remained silent for a moment, and wrote a word beneath it: "Confirmed."
Later, an informal appendix to the Ark's voyage history compiled a volume titled "Ke's Graveyard: A Compilation of Sightings," collecting hundreds of similar observation records spanning millions of years. Each entry was extremely brief. One of them was only three words: "So empty, ah."
________________________________________
Chapter 12
The Era of Silence ended about twenty thousand years after launch. This day was marked as the "First Anomalous Event."
The automated analysis system flagged a region that deviated from statistical expectations: an area of several thousand square kilometers on the hull surface where reconnection impact frequency was about seven parts per million higher than normal. Over the previous nearly twenty thousand years, the maximum deviation had never exceeded two parts per million.
They were passing by some external structure with definite spatial extension. After twenty thousand years of silent voyage, the Ark had encountered its first other membrane.
The discovery of the external membrane triggered a minor social tremor inside the Ark. It was the sudden return of hope.
Over the following centuries, the Ark's monitoring department conducted detailed passive observation of the external membrane. It was larger than the Ark but far smaller than the Mother Membrane, and the pattern of its surface defects showed remarkable regularity, indicating it was a mature membrane that had undergone long evolution.
The debate centered on whether to initiate active contact. The debate lasted about five hundred years. The external membrane did not wait.
In the third century of passive observation, the external membrane's reconnection impact pattern began to show large, irregular fluctuations. The Ark's theoretical department proposed several possible explanations. The most unsettling one: the external membrane might be disintegrating.
The Ark's trajectory was urgently adjusted to a safe distance from any disintegration shockwave. The external membrane did not disintegrate—it underwent a "near-disintegration event" and eventually returned to a stable state. Later analysis indicated that naturally emergent membranes possessed stronger self-repair capabilities than artificially constructed ones.
The technical summary in the Ark's voyage log was a single sentence: "We have encountered another membrane. In this lightless ocean, we are not the only vortex."
________________________________________
Volume V: Voyage Atlas
Chapter 13
After the external membrane event, the monitoring department encoded impact pattern signatures into the automated detection algorithm. Over the following tens of millions of years, the Ark's sensor network recorded tens of thousands of similar events. The vast majority were brief, distant passings-by; a few were close-range, long-duration parallel voyages—the Ark and another membrane sailing in the substrate at similar speeds and directions, paralleling for up to hundreds of thousands of years.
The observational data revealed a crucial fact: membranes were not isolated islands. They were interconnected. Membranes in the substrate formed a network—a network connected by long-range influences. This network transmitted no light, no matter, but it transmitted existence itself. The Ark sailed within this network.
During this period, the Ark underwent three technological leaps. The first was "passive navigation upgrade"—learning to infer the spatial distribution of substrate circulation stress fields. The second was "active stress navigation"—learning to send controllable reconnection event waves into the substrate and, through their echoes, construct three-dimensional position maps of external membranes. The third was the "stress corridor strategy"—discovering low-stress channels within the substrate circulation stress fields; sailing along these channels significantly extended hull maintenance cycles, prolonging the expected voyage timescale from billions of years to hundreds of billions of years.
The Ark's core navigation manual summarized this history in one sentence: "We learned to see without looking, to move without motion, to arrive without waiting."
________________________________________
Chapter 14: The Long-Order Star
About eighty million years into the voyage, the over four thousand ecological units inside the Ark had evolved into vastly different civilizational forms. Some had regressed to agrarian societies, some had developed technologies more advanced than those of the Mother Membrane's Awakening Epoch, and some, in repeated cycles of rise and fall, had forgotten they came from the Ark.
The Long-Order Star was the anchor for all civilizations inside the Ark. It was responsible for maintaining the stability of the gravity grid—making the suns rise on time, keeping planets in their orbits, preventing the membrane surface structure from drifting due to minute entropy increases. Its technological level represented an absolute suppression across orders of magnitude over any civilization inside the Ark. It took no part in inter-civilizational politics, cared not who conquered whom or who allied with whom. In the eyes of the Long-Order Star, these were merely faint noise on the membrane surface structure.
A civilizational region called the "Crimson Corona Domain" rose over millions of years to become the most powerful force inside the Ark. They had mastered local gravity manipulation technology independent of the Long-Order Star's control system. Over hundreds of millennia, the influence of the Crimson Corona Domain came to cover nearly one-third of the Continuation Sector's star territories. Their technology grew ever more mature, edging ever closer to the core domain of the Long-Order Star—that forbidden zone marked on the first page of every star chart. The long ages had caused most civilizations to forget what it signified. The Crimson Corona Domain prepared to probe it.
At the annual briefing, the Chief Strategy Officer of the Crimson Corona Domain spent three minutes mentioning the "Long-Order Star Forbidden Zone." Those three minutes were placed at the end of the meeting, near the tea break. The attendees looked down at their terminals, each handling their own stellar crop quotas and fleet movements.
No one looked up.
After the meeting, a young staff officer caught up with the chief: "Sir, regarding the Long-Order Star... shouldn't we conduct a special study?"
The chief patted him on the shoulder: "Child, that is a myth from twenty million years ago. Have you read the original texts of the myth? They say our ancestors fled here from a larger universe. Do you think this planet—" he pointed out the window at the blue-and-green mother planet, "—came here by 'fleeing'? We have flourished here for eight million years. We are indigenous."
The young staff officer returned to his desk and pulled up the public file on the "Long-Order Star." The entire public record amounted to less than four pages; the latest entry, updated seven hundred years ago, contained only: "Annual status: Stable. No anomalies."
He closed the terminal.
Sixty years later, the Crimson Corona Domain's first reconnaissance fleet ignited its engines at the edge of the Long-Order Star forbidden zone. Their goal was not to touch the hull membrane surface—they had not even thought of touching the hull. They merely wanted to measure whether the ancient "authority" of the Long-Order Star still had substance. There was a gravitational monitoring station of the Long-Order Star within the forbidden zone; they believed that if they could just bypass a single signal relay node of that station, they could prove the Long-Order Star was not omnipotent.
On the seventh day after entering the forbidden zone, the fleet reached the designated coordinates. They released a set of passive detectors, preparing to bypass the routine scanning windows of the Long-Order Star monitoring station.
The detectors operated for less than three seconds.
The Long-Order Star's central gravity computation engine logged the intrusion. The log entry read:
Timestamp: Voyage Era 79,842,113, Cycle 47, Day 3, Local Time 02:17:04.003
Event Type: Unauthorized Probe Node Access
Coordinates: Long-Order Star Forbidden Zone – Edge – Relay Node G-7
Operation Method: Remote Weave Interruption
Operation Target: Crimson Corona Domain Primary Star KF-CrimsonCorona-001
Operation Result: Subject deconstructed. Discrete particle cloud diffusion radius: 0.34 light-years. Estimated full dissipation in fifty thousand years.
Operation Basis: Reference Protocol TDMU-Hull Safety-Article 7 (Non-Bypass Clause)
Remarks: None.
No broadcast. No warning. No communication in any form to the survivors of the Crimson Corona Domain as to "why."
The Long-Order Star's logic was simple: If you can touch this switch, you already know the answer. If you don't know the answer, you shouldn't have touched it.
On the midnight the Crimson Corona Primary Star went out, that star which had burned for hundreds of millions of years, in a single microsecond, had all the weave relations among its elementary particles dissolved. The star went from a burning plasma sphere to a discrete particle cloud floating in space. No light. No heat. No sound.
The capital planet, in the instant it lost its sun, entered an eternal black night.
In the same microsecond, the Long-Order Star's central gravity computation engine did something else. It recalculated the optimal distribution path within the Ark's gravitational field for the particle cloud released by the dismantled star.
The calculation results were output three seconds later: the particle cloud would be slowly drawn by the gravitational field to a region of higher defect density on the hull surface, to serve as a reserve material depot for reinforcing the membrane surface over the coming tens of thousands of years.
The system automatically assigned this material depot a designation: RM-CrimsonCorona-001.
This designation followed the exact same naming convention used during the Mother Membrane era for dismantled masquerade stars. The system did not record "Is this appropriate." The system recorded only "Is this optimal."
The remnant fleets of the Crimson Corona Domain scattered to every corner of the Ark over the following millennia. Once the most powerful force inside the Ark, they were now a group of exiles without a homeworld, without a sun, surviving only by their wandering fleets. Their ships flew a black flag at their sterns. The color of their extinguished sun.
On an observation planet forty thousand light-years from the Crimson Corona Domain, a young astronomer's telescope captured that sudden "new star"—a thousand times brighter than a normal supernova, yet it faded within hours.
He checked every known stellar evolution model; none could explain such a light curve.
He handed the data to his advisor. The advisor glanced at the screen and turned the telescope in another direction.
"Don't look at it," the advisor said. "That's the Long-Order Star tidying up."
"What do you mean, 'tidying up'?"
The advisor was silent for a long time.
"It means they felt some things had been left out too long, and it was time to put them away."
That night, the office of the planetary governor received an intelligence summary from forty thousand light-years away. The summary contained only one line:
"The Long-Order Star is real."
This summary was then encrypted and distributed to the highest decision-making levels of all major civilizations in that stellar region.
For the next approximately six million years, no civilization conducted any active probing within the Long-Order Star forbidden zone.
What they feared was not just the punishment itself. They feared that they would never know whose turn it would be to be "tidied up" next.
"Doing a Crimson Corona" later became a slang phrase inside the Ark, meaning "overreaching and self-destructing." "The Long-Order Star is watching us" became one of the most common sayings.
The Long-Order Star remained silent. It simply continued to maintain the stability of the gravity grid, continued to make the suns rise on time.
The Ark's core memory's record of this event was extremely terse, just three lines: "Approximately eighty million years of the Voyage Era. The Crimson Corona Domain conducted unauthorized probing of the Long-Order Star forbidden zone. The Long-Order Star judged it a threat to order. Punishment executed. Crimson Corona Primary Star dismantled. No additional measures."
No one signed it. Archive classification: "Routine Maintenance."
________________________________________
Volume VI: Arrival
Chapter 15
The exact moment of the target membrane's discovery is precisely recorded in the voyage log: approximately one hundred and seventy-three million years after launch.
The active stress navigation system detected a signal of extremely high stability. The membrane was of immense scale, on the same order of magnitude as the Mother Membrane. Its surface defect distribution was highly regular, with an estimated defect fraction below one-thousandth. Its internal spatial network was still actively operating. Total age: approximately 9.7 billion years—about 4.1 billion years younger than the Mother Membrane. Estimated remaining lifespan: between nine and fifteen billion years.
Most importantly: its internal spatial network's structural parameters showed statistically significant consistency with the Ark's own weave pattern in three key dimensions, meaning that life from inside the Ark could survive directly within the target membrane without any physical adaptation process.
The council's vote took about three years. It passed unanimously. The Ark began to adjust its course.
________________________________________
Chapter 16
The course adjustment was a long and delicate process. The Ark's inertia was immense; its energy reserves came from the final round of stellar dismantling before launch, stored in the hull's weave structure in the form of elastic potential energy. Over nearly two hundred million years of voyage, this energy had been gradually released.
The deceleration upon approaching the target membrane was divided into three stages: using the target membrane's gravitational field as a braking medium; controlled micro-deformation of the hull to release energy; and final precision docking.
In the last few years before docking, the Continuation Sector entered a full readiness state. The voyage log entry three days before docking recorded:
"Distance remaining to contact point: 0.00074 light-years. Relative velocity: decelerated below safety threshold. Target membrane surface defect distribution map completed. Our defect matching pattern optimized. All systems self-checked. All ready.
"The vast majority of this generation of Ark residents were born during the voyage. They have never seen the Mother Membrane. To them, the universe is the enclosed space bounded by the inner surface of the hull. They know from the education system that a larger universe exists outside the hull. Knowing and believing are two different things.
"After docking is complete, they will see light for the first time—light from another universe."
________________________________________
Chapter 17
The docking was completed at the scheduled time.
Zero point three seconds. The surface defects of the two membranes meshed perfectly at the moment the connection bridge was established. The Ark's hull was no longer sealed—at the docking point, the Ark's weave network and the target membrane's spatial network formed a seamless connection. Two universes became one at the point of contact.
The first signal was light.
Billions of individuals simultaneously saw something they had never seen in their lives. The darkness ended. The grey, structureless "Surface of the Sea" vanished. In its place was light—stars, galaxies, a complete, active, burning universe.
The gravity grid starry sky inside the Ark was still there, but behind it, a new background had lit up—the universe of the Second Membrane. The two layers of stars overlapped again, just as they had long ago, in the time of the Mother Membrane.
In the first few hours after docking, the Ark's monitoring department completed basic parameter measurements of the new membrane. The speed of light and the fine-structure constant were both within the adaptive range. Elemental abundance distributions were broadly similar to the Mother Membrane, but there were two additional islands of stability in the heavy nuclei region.
Spatial coordinates confirmed: the Ark's docking point was in the peripheral region of a medium-sized galaxy cluster in the Second Membrane. The overall scale of the Second Membrane was roughly equivalent to the Mother Membrane; its total age was about 9.7 billion years. Projected remaining lifespan: median estimate approximately twelve billion years.
On the first day after docking, the director of the Ark's monitoring department wrote in the voyage log: "We do not know if this membrane has native life. We do not know if it has civilization. We do not know if it will accept us. But we do know one thing: we are no longer the Ark civilization. From today, we are a dual-membrane civilization—a civilization spanning two universes. The Mother Membrane lived 13.8 billion years and used its final 317,000 years to gestate us. The Second Membrane has already lived 9.7 billion years, and still has twelve billion years ahead. We have brought the legacy of the Mother Membrane here. We will take root here."
________________________________________
Chapter 18
Regarding subsequent events after arrival in the Second Membrane, the records in the Ark's core memory are still being updated. The following is the last formal log entry as of the current record time:
"We have been operating inside the Second Membrane for approximately forty thousand years. The following work has been completed.
"First, full unsealing of the Ark's Continuation Sector. All ecological units have established secure connections with the Second Membrane's spatial network. Ecosystems are gradually adapting to the physical constants of the new membrane. Of the ten billion residents, approximately thirty percent have chosen to establish habitation units in suitable galaxies of the Second Membrane.
"Second, preliminary surveys of the Second Membrane have been initiated. Confirmed at least seven independently originated life zones within the Second Membrane—three at the microbial stage, two at the multicellular stage, one at an early technological civilization stage, and one at a technological stage comparable to the mid-Awakening Epoch of the Mother Membrane. Contact with the last civilizational zone has not yet been established; contact protocols are under review.
"Third, the first batch of interpretive tests of Memory Sector information has been completed. We placed stored information from the Mother Membrane civilization in a lifeless galaxy in the Second Membrane, left it undisturbed for about ten thousand years, and retrieved it. Information integrity test result: 99.9997%. The Second Membrane's spatial network environment is highly compatible with the Mother Membrane. Our information can persist here. An's encoding has withstood the test of cross-universal transmission.
"Fourth, the 'Horizon' system has completed its calibration in the new environment. The complete legacy of the approximately 120 million civilizations stored within—languages, histories, art, sciences—are all readable. We have examined the index catalogue. Of the 120 million files, every last page reads the same line: 'Civilization Continuation Status: Terminated.' Only two files are exceptions. One is NGC-447SS2-S7, with Gu Lin's signature in the notes field—'Permanently removed from dismantling sequence.' The other is KD-9A901, with Shen Yuan's handwritten note in the notes field—'Seven confirmed intelligent civilizations. No alternative available. Signatory assumes full responsibility.' Among all the files, only these two records are different. 120 million. Two.
"Fifth, conceptual research on the next-generation Ark has been initiated. The Second Membrane will eventually disintegrate. Postulate Twelve has no exceptions. There is no urgency at present—but urgency is something that should not be waited for. In the Mother Membrane era, we had only 317,000 years from the terminal data to disintegration, barely enough. In the Second Membrane era, we have billions of years. When the terminal data of the Second Membrane arrives, we will no longer be panicked fugitives. We will be calm migrators.
"Sixth, the contact strategy review has reached an impasse. Two opinions cannot be reconciled.
"One opinion holds: The Second Membrane already has mature native civilizations. The Ark should keep a low profile and integrate slowly in the peripheral regions. We are survivors, not conquerors.
"The other opinion holds: The Second Membrane has only twelve billion years of remaining lifespan. If the Ark cannot secure a sufficient share in resource allocation, the construction of the next-generation Ark will not be completed before disintegration. We have already made harder choices in the Mother Membrane. If we yield in the resource competition today out of moral qualms, then those dismantled galaxies—those suns we extinguished—their sacrifice loses all meaning.
"The core disagreement between the two views is a direct continuation of Gu Lin's choice regarding S7 and Shen Yuan's choice regarding KD-9A901 during the construction: sacrifice some to save more, or refuse to sacrifice any, but potentially lose all in the end? In the Mother Membrane era, we had the countdown of the terminal data forcing our choice. Now there is no countdown. We must decide, in complete freedom, what kind of people we are.
"The Mother Membrane used 13.8 billion years to teach us how long a universe can be. The terminal data used 317,000 years to teach us how tight time can be. The Ark used 173 million years to teach us how far a voyage can go. The Second Membrane uses its twelve billion years of remaining lifespan to teach us how wide the future can be. The Long-Order Star used one star to teach us what order is. Gu Lin taught us to spare one. Shen Yuan taught us to choose between seven and many more—and what a signature means.
"We still have time. We will not waste it."
________________________________________
Epilogue
In the deepest recesses of the Ark's core memory is preserved a document. No title, no signature, no date. In the catalogue, it is marked "Record of the Toad Imagery—Original Version."
The content of the document:
"At the bottom of a pond on a summer night, a toad lies still in the water, blowing bubbles from its mouth. The bubbles rise, expand, deform, oscillate, and ultimately burst, returning to ripples in the water.
"Water is the substrate—the underlying layer that bears everything. There may be other bubbles in the water, each independently rising, expanding, and bursting. No direct sound transmits between bubbles, but they are all in the same water, and their tremors pass through the water.
"The bubble wall is the universe membrane—an ordered structure emerging from the substrate. The rising of the bubble is time—irreversible unfolding. The expansion of the bubble is the cooling and evolution of the universe. The texture on the bubble is matter—the weave structures on the membrane. The bursting of the bubble is the final end—the structure disintegrates and returns to the substrate.
"The toad remains still in the unreachable depths of the substrate. We are born from the ripples on the membrane, and can never look back at it in our lifetime—this does not prevent us from reconstructing its appearance, step by step, through the imprints on the membrane.
"Meaning is not conserved; only reconnection persists without end."
Directly beneath the document is a line of annotation added later. The encoding method differs from the original, clearly added long afterward.
"These are words written by our ancestors in the Mother Membrane era. At that time, they had not yet built the Ark, had not yet experienced the voyage, had not yet arrived at the Second Membrane. They simply looked at the night sky and imagined what lay beyond the universe.
"Now we all know. Beyond the universe are more universes. Beyond the universe is the Bedrock Sea. Beyond the universe is silence, darkness, and ceaseless fluctuations.
"But we also know another thing: After the bubble bursts, the water remains. The bubble can re-emerge in another part of the water. The toad is still blowing bubbles. We do not know how many more bubbles are rising, and perhaps we will never know. But we will continue the voyage. Continue building. Continue recording.
"The Mother Membrane's terminal data gave us 317,000 years. The Second Membrane gives us twelve billion years. Time is the fruit of civilization, a gift, a material. We use it to build. To voyage. To continue."
The last line of the annotation contains only two words. These two words are set as the foundational core words of the Ark civilization in all education systems inside the Ark. Every child born within the Ark learns these two words as their first words—not mother, not father, but these two words.
Keep walking.
(End of Saga of Survival on the Membrane)
